xpanding upon  so basically from the look of the  trailer it looks as though  this first season of the foundation  series at least  is going to be about the fall of trantor  itself which is something that's not  actually depicted  in the book so you have the beginning  opening  we have gail dornick and you have harry  selden uh on the planet trantor and  they're petitioning the emperor and tel  and to start the foundation because they  know through  mathematical cycle history that the  empire is headed towards a decline and  essentially a crash and harry selden has  a plan to save it so  it gets pretty complicated from there  but essentially the book  kind of ends and that first section with  harry selden  being allowed to make the foundation  which is basically  explained to the emperor as a place  where people will go  to write down everything in the universe  ever like the history of the entire  universe the encyclopedia galactica  so that they will be able to keep all  the information in the universe and that  will be the way of maintaining the  history of humanity and ensuring the  survival of the empire essentially but  that's not harry's actual plan his plan  his  real plan is actually way more  complicated and we're not going to get  into that but that's the essential  gist of like what he's setting up for  the emperor  so looking at this show it is  definitely taking some liberties with  the story  it is definitely borrowing in my opinion  from the work of frank herbert  in several ways uh i'm getting some  tylaxu vibes with the genetic  engineering that seems to be a part of  the show that's not in the books  um and i'm also getting a dune vibe with  this whole family structure that they're  setting up with the  leader of the empire now because that's  not really a thing  in the the original foundation book  it we don't really we don't see the  emperor's family it's not about them  it's not about the characters so it  seems here that they're  pulling oh i 